To avoid calling `find_any_set_near` more than necessary, use `max_hint()` to get the maximum number of iterations that are valid for the current bitset. The value returned by `max_hint()` tells you how many times the `find_any_set_near` function should be called in a search loop to avoid redundant or unnecessary calls.

Example usage:

```cpp
unsigned hint = initial_position;
unsigned max_calls = bitset.max_hint();

for (unsigned i = 0; i < max_calls; ++i) {
  auto result = bitset.find_any_set_near(hint);
  if (result.first) {
    // Found a set bit at position result.second
    break;
  }
  // Update hint to result.second for the next search
  hint = result.second;
}
```

This loop ensures that `find_any_set_near` is called at most `max_hint()` times. If a set bit is found earlier, the loop exits early.

Yes, copying between Kokkos Views with incompatible layouts requires a temporary View as an intermediary. Here is a minimal example using `Kokkos::deep_copy` and a temporary View:

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    Kokkos::initialize(argc, argv);
    {
        int N = 1000;
        int R = 5;

        // Views with incompatible layouts
        Kokkos::View<int**[5], Kokkos::LayoutLeft> d_view("DeviceView", N, R);
        Kokkos::View<int**[5], Kokkos::LayoutRight, Kokkos::HostSpace> h_view("HostView", N, R);

        // Create a temporary view with the same layout as d_view
        auto temp_view = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(d_view);

        // Copy from h_view to temp_view (both accessible by the host)
        Kokkos::deep_copy(temp_view, h_view);

        // Copy from temp_view to d_view (now layout compatible)
        Kokkos::deep_copy(d_view, temp_view);
    }
    Kokkos::finalize();
}
```

This ensures compatibility during the copy by using a temporary view that shares the same layout as the destination.

The `Kokkos::DualView` requires you to explicitly mark a memory space as modified using `modify<Host>()` before calling `sync<Device>()` because the `sync()` method is designed to be efficient and only copies data when necessary. It checks the "modified" flags to determine if a copy is required.

When you modify data on the host, you must explicitly call `modify<Host>()` to indicate that the host data has changed and needs to be synchronized with the device. Without calling `modify<Host>()`, the `sync<Device>()` call assumes no changes were made and skips the copy.

To ensure proper synchronization after modifying host data, follow this pattern:

```cpp
// Modify data on the host
// ...

// Mark host data as modified
dualView.modify<Host>();

// Sync data to device
dualView.sync<Device>();
```

This guarantees that `sync<Device>()` will copy the updated data from the host to the device.

