To compute a gradient penalty using higher-order gradients in C++ with PyTorch, you can use the `torch::autograd::grad` function to calculate the gradient of the output with respect to the input, and then apply a penalty based on the norm of the gradient. Here's an example of how to do it:

```cpp
#include <torch/torch.h>

int main() {
  // Define a model
  auto model = torch::nn::Linear(4, 3);

  // Create an input tensor with gradient tracking enabled
  auto input = torch::randn({3, 4}).requires_grad_(true);
  auto output = model(input);

  // Define a target and calculate the loss
  auto target = torch::randn({3, 3});
  auto loss = torch::nn::MSELoss()(output, target);

  // Compute gradients
  auto grad_output = torch::ones_like(output);
  auto gradient = torch::autograd::grad(
      {output}, 
      {input}, 
      /*grad_outputs=*/{grad_output}, 
      /*create_graph=*/true
  )[0];

  // Compute the gradient penalty
  auto gradient_penalty = torch::pow((gradient.norm(2, /*dim=*/1) - 1), 2).mean();

  // Combine the loss with the gradient penalty
  auto combined_loss = loss + gradient_penalty;
  combined_loss.backward();

  // Print the gradient of the input
  std::cout << input.grad() << std::endl;

  return 0;
}
```

This code snippet demonstrates how to compute the gradient of the output with respect to the input using `torch::autograd::grad`, and then computes a gradient penalty based on the norm of the gradient. The gradient penalty is then added to the loss function before calling `backward()` to compute gradients for training.

